1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,206
Second-order linear equations with constant coefficients are

2
00:00:04,206 --> 00:00:08,180
the equations behind springs, circuits, and vibrations.

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:12,973
From Lecture 9, you already know the shape of the answer.

4
00:00:12,973 --> 00:00:16,806
Find two independent solutions, y one and y two, and the

5
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:20,460
general solution is c one y one, plus c two y two.

6
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:24,859
So the only real question is: how do we find y one and y two?

7
00:00:24,859 --> 00:00:28,616
For constant coefficients, there is a beautiful shortcut.

8
00:00:32,216 --> 00:00:34,269
Guess y equals e to the m x.

9
00:00:34,269 --> 00:00:37,789
Every derivative just brings out another factor of m.

10
00:00:38,209 --> 00:00:42,167
Plug those in. Every term has e to the m x, so factor it out.

11
00:00:42,587 --> 00:00:44,784
But e to the m x is never zero.

12
00:00:44,784 --> 00:00:46,808
So the bracket must be zero.

13
00:00:46,808 --> 00:00:49,583
That quadratic is the auxiliary equation.

14
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:52,705
Some books call it the characteristic equation.

15
00:00:53,125 --> 00:00:55,575
Lecture 9 had this exact case.

16
00:00:55,575 --> 00:00:58,687
y double prime minus nine y equals zero.

17
00:00:58,687 --> 00:01:01,733
The auxiliary equation is m squared minus nine

18
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:05,441
equals zero, so m is three or negative three.

19
00:01:05,861 --> 00:01:09,798
Which gives e to the three x, and e to the negative three x.

20
00:01:09,798 --> 00:01:12,737
Exactly the fundamental set from that lecture.

21
00:01:16,437 --> 00:01:19,120
A quadratic has three possibilities, and each

22
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:21,638
one gives a different form of solution.

23
00:01:21,638 --> 00:01:24,923
Check the discriminant, b squared minus four a c.

24
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:28,373
Case one. Two distinct real roots.

25
00:01:28,373 --> 00:01:33,235
Then y equals c one e to the m one x, plus c two e to the m two x.

26
00:01:33,235 --> 00:01:34,813
Pure exponentials.

27
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:37,888
Case two. One repeated root.

28
00:01:37,888 --> 00:01:43,136
You only get one exponential, so you need a second independent solution.

29
00:01:43,136 --> 00:01:48,699
Multiply by x. y equals c one e to the m x, plus c two x e to the m x.

30
00:01:49,119 --> 00:01:53,873
Case three. Complex roots, alpha plus or minus i beta.

31
00:01:53,873 --> 00:01:58,231
Using Euler's formula from Lecture 8, the solution is e to the

32
00:01:58,231 --> 00:02:03,117
alpha x, times c one cosine beta x, plus c two sine beta x.

33
00:02:03,117 --> 00:02:06,617
An oscillation inside an exponential envelope.

34
00:02:07,037 --> 00:02:09,185
Where does that come from?

35
00:02:09,185 --> 00:02:14,848
Euler's formula says e to the i beta x is cosine beta x, plus i sine beta x.

36
00:02:14,848 --> 00:02:18,754
The real and imaginary parts are each real solutions.

37
00:02:22,655 --> 00:02:25,470
Let us solve an initial value problem.

38
00:02:25,470 --> 00:02:31,161
Four y double prime, plus four y prime, plus seventeen y, equals zero, with

39
00:02:31,161 --> 00:02:35,977
y of zero equal to negative one, and y prime of zero equal to two.

40
00:02:36,396 --> 00:02:42,202
The auxiliary equation is four m squared, plus four m, plus seventeen, equals zero.

41
00:02:42,623 --> 00:02:47,538
Quadratic formula. m equals negative four, plus or minus the square root

42
00:02:47,538 --> 00:02:50,854
of sixteen minus two seventy two, over eight.

43
00:02:50,854 --> 00:02:54,940
That is negative four plus or minus sixteen i, over eight.

44
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:59,688
So m is negative one half, plus or minus two i. Complex.

45
00:02:59,688 --> 00:03:02,904
Alpha is negative one half, and beta is two.

46
00:03:03,323 --> 00:03:07,642
General solution: y equals e to the negative x over two,

47
00:03:07,642 --> 00:03:11,623
times c one cosine two x, plus c two sine two x.

48
00:03:12,043 --> 00:03:14,983
Apply y of zero equals negative one.

49
00:03:14,983 --> 00:03:20,589
Cosine zero is one and sine zero is zero, so c one equals negative one.

50
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:24,207
For y prime, use the product rule carefully.

51
00:03:24,207 --> 00:03:29,846
At x equals zero you get, negative one half c one, plus two c two, equals two.

52
00:03:29,846 --> 00:03:31,822
So c two is three quarters.

53
00:03:32,242 --> 00:03:37,259
Final answer. y equals e to the negative x over two, times negative

54
00:03:37,259 --> 00:03:40,861
cosine two x, plus three quarters sine two x.

55
00:03:41,281 --> 00:03:43,595
And that is a damped oscillation.

56
00:03:43,595 --> 00:03:47,530
It starts at negative one, heads up with slope two, and dies

57
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:51,059
away inside the envelope e to the negative x over two.

58
00:03:51,059 --> 00:03:53,431
Exactly what a damped spring does.

59
00:03:57,731 --> 00:03:58,614
Three traps.

60
00:03:59,034 --> 00:04:02,277
One. A repeated root without the x.

61
00:04:02,277 --> 00:04:07,440
c one e to the m x plus c two e to the m x is really only one solution.

62
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:10,418
You must multiply the second one by x.

63
00:04:10,838 --> 00:04:16,210
Two. Complex roots. Beta goes inside the cosine and sine, without the i.

64
00:04:16,210 --> 00:04:19,201
And do not drop the e to the alpha x out front.

65
00:04:19,621 --> 00:04:23,804
Three. Rushing the derivative for the second initial condition.

66
00:04:23,804 --> 00:04:27,389
Use the product rule on e to the alpha x times the bracket.

67
00:04:31,189 --> 00:04:32,453
Here is the recipe.

68
00:04:32,873 --> 00:04:35,023
One. Write the auxiliary equation.

69
00:04:35,443 --> 00:04:38,283
Two. Distinct real roots give two exponentials.

70
00:04:38,703 --> 00:04:41,321
Three. A repeated root needs the extra x.

71
00:04:41,741 --> 00:04:46,485
Four. Complex roots give an exponential envelope, times cosine and sine.

72
00:04:46,905 --> 00:04:50,034
Five. Apply the initial conditions, carefully.

73
00:04:50,034 --> 00:04:53,946
And that completes all eight archetypes. Good luck on the midterm.
